Here is the code snippet for the `toJavaVersionIntArray` method:
```java
private static int[] toJavaVersionIntArray(String version, int limit) {
    if (version == null) {
        return ArrayUtils.EMPTY_INT_ARRAY;
    }
    String[] strings = Pattern.compile("[^\\d]").split(version);
    int[] ints = new int[Math.min(limit, strings.length)];
    int j = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < strings.length && j < limit; i++) {
        String s = strings[i];
        if (s.length() > 0) {
            ints[j++] = Integer.parseInt(s);
        }
    }
    return ints;
}
```

The issue described in the bug report may be related to how the `version` string is processed in the `toJavaVersionIntArray` method. The `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` mentioned could occur if the input `version` string does not match the expected format when splitting and parsing into integers in this method.

To further investigate the root cause, we should analyze how the `toJavaVersionIntArray` method handles the input `version` string, especially in scenarios where the `version` is null or has unexpected characters.

